Single-molecule magnets (SMMs) and single-chain magnets (SCMs), also known as molecular nanomagnets, are molecular species of nanoscale proportions with the potential for high information storage density and spintronics applications. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are three-dimensional ordered assemblies of inorganic nodes and organic linkers, featuring structural diversity and multiple chemical and physical properties. The concept of using these frameworks as scaffolds in the study of molecular nanomagnets provides an opportunity to constrain the local coordination geometries of lanthanide centers and organize the individual magnetic building blocks (MBBs, including both transition-metal and lanthanide MBBs) into topologically well-defined arrays that represent two key factors governing the magnetic properties of molecular nanomagnets. In this tutorial review, we summarize recent progress in this newly emerging field.
## Key learning points
(1) Research into molecular nanomagnets can be performed using high-dimensional frameworks.   
(2) A promising synthetic strategy towards molecular nanomagnets involves constraining the coordination geometries in framework structures.   
(3) Diverse assemblies of magnetic building blocks can form high-dimensional frameworks.   
(4) The magnetic properties of high-dimensional frameworks are tunable.   
(5) MOFs provide future challenges and opportunities for molecular nanomagnets.
## 1. Introduction
The past two decades have witnessed worldwide research interest in both molecular nanomagnets and metal–organic frameworks, which could be married to generate a new research field because they both belong to the field of coordination chemistry. Molecular nanomagnets, namely single-molecule magnets (SMMs) and single-chain magnets (SCMs), are magnetically anisotropic discrete molecules or molecular chains that can be regarded as nanomagnets at relatively low temperatures.1,2 The magnetic bistability and the molecular nature of SMMs/SCMs give them great potential in high-density information storage, spintronics and quantum computing.3
Characteristic physical features of molecular nanomagnets are magnetization dynamics (or slow relaxation of magnetization) and hysteresis loops, which originate from the magnetic bistability; however, these are only accessible at cryogenic temperatures because thermal agitation can make the magnetic bistability undetectable, which severely limits their practical applications. Therefore, improving working temperatures via rational design with an understanding of how the energy barrier for magnetization reversal and the magnetic interactions between spin carriers correlate with the above two features has become one of the most important issues in this field. In fact, for molecular nanomagnets, the magnetization dynamics significantly govern the hysteresis loops; this will be discussed in detail in the following section.
From a structural point of view, any physical property originates from the electronic structure of a material. Understanding magneto-structural correlations can greatly contribute to the design and synthesis of new molecular nanomagnets with enhanced properties. Several key factors can influence the magnetization dynamics of molecular nanomagnets, such as the separation of the ground and excited magnetic states, the magnetic exchange interactions, magnetic dipolar interactions and hyperfine interactions.4 After almost one decade of studies on transition-metal-based SMMs, lanthanide ions have been widely chosen as excellent spin carriers for SMMs due to their inherent single-ion anisotropies derived from strong spin–orbit couplings. Although the inner 4f orbitals may not interact strongly with ligands, appropriate ligand fields with specific symmetry (vide infra) can greatly enhance their magnetic anisotropies, which has been well demonstrated in mononuclear SMMs (or single-ion magnets, SIMs). To date, the record highest energy barrier for magnetization reversal is $226~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for transitionmetal SMMs5 and $652~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for lanthanide SMMs,6 and the highest temperature for the observation of a hysteresis loop is $14\mathrm{~K~}$ .7
On the other hand, research into metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) has been flourishing in recent years due to their potentially ultra-high porosity and extraordinary structural diversity.8 MOFs can be regarded as assemblies of inorganic nodes and organic linkers interconnected by coordination bonds, which is actually the definition of a coordination polymer, except that the structural order is three-dimensional. The diversity of the nodes and the linkers lays a foundation of abundant structures, tunable pores and functionalities. The porosity of MOFs has shown great benefits with respect to applications in gas storage and separation, catalysis and sensing; however, the magnetic properties of the metal nodes within MOFs are not well documented. If appropriate coordination symmetry is formed around the metal centers and/or the metal centers are magnetically coupled, SMM or SCM behavior could be observed in MOFs. In addition, the magnetization dynamics of the metal nodes could be fine-tuned via perturbation of single-ion anisotropy and magnetic interactions by modification of the coordination geometries of the metal nodes and the linkers in the frameworks.
Based on the aforementioned two research fields, chemical assembly of SMMs or SCMs as magnetic building blocks (MBBs) in MOFs is expected to be a promising strategy towards new molecular nanomagnets for the following reasons: (i) the coordination symmetries around metal centers (especially for lanthanide ions) can be fixed in rigid frameworks through rational structural design; (ii) organizing the individual magnetic
