## 2. Constraining the coordination geometries of lanthanide centers in MOFs
The magnetization dynamics of lanthanide SMMs mainly originate from single-ion behavior because it is difficult to build effective pathways for magnetic interactions between lanthanide centers due to the nature of inner f electrons. This situation is different from the magnetic relaxation mechanism of transition-metalbased SMMs, in which both the spin and exchange interactions contribute simultaneously to the magnetization dynamics. In MOFs, transition-metal-based coordination clusters function as nodes; however, due to the high symmetry of the cluster, the magnetic anisotropy is weak and hence they do not usually behave as molecular nanomagnets, except when the spins are strongly coupled along one direction to cause them to behave as SCMs (the examples are rather rare, vide infra). As an alternative candidate, lanthanide centers that are usually weakly coupled in MOFs have shown obvious advantages with respect to molecular nanomagnet studies because large magnetization reversal energy barriers can be achieved via symmetry-related single-ion anisotropy originating from strong spin–orbit coupling and crystal field effects.
The complexity of the electronic structure of f-elements is a huge obstacle to clearly presenting the factors governing the magnetic behavior of lanthanide SMMs because the intrinsic effects of lanthanide ions, including the electronic repulsions, spin–orbit couplings, crystal-field effects, magnetic exchange and dipolar couplings, although relatively weak, can contribute significantly to the magnetization dynamics. One of the promising ways of better understanding the magnetization dynamics of f-element SMMs is to systematically change the coordination symmetry and strength of the crystal field via rational ligand design and crystal engineering. In this context, the construction of lanthanide MOFs is a new and useful platform, not only because of their structural diversity, but also their structural flexibility, making it possible to chemically tune the coordination symmetries of lanthanide centers for SMM studies (Table 1).
In general, both the exchange interactions and dipolar interactions between lanthanide ions are weak due to the nature of inner f-electrons but they can influence the relaxation rate
