## 2.1 Significance of the coordination geometries of lanthanide ions for magnetization dynamics
For free tripositive lanthanide ions, the orbital momentum is unquenched and anisotropic except in LaIII, GdIII and LuIII.13 Based on Russell–Saunders coupling, the orbital moment $L$ is coupled with spin $s$ to generate ground term $^{2S+1}L_{J},$ which contains $2J+1$ degenerate $m_{J}$ substates. In a crystal field, the degeneracy of $m_{J}$ substates is split by the electrostatic effects of the crystal field, resulting in $J+1/2$ pairs of doublets $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{J}}$ is half-integer) or $J$ pairs of doublets and one singlet $\boldsymbol{\mathit{\Omega}}(\boldsymbol{J}$ is integer). The bistable nature of the $m_{J}$ doublets provides the fundamental basis of lanthanide SMMs, while the energy gaps between the doublets primarily determine the energy barrier for magnetization reorientation. To maximize the energy barrier, one of the most useful strategies is to match an appropriate symmetry ligand field according to the shape of the 4f-shell electron distribution, as proposed by Rinehart and Long.14
In general, the Hamiltonian of a crystal field contribution based on Stevens’ theory can be written as
$$
\hat{H}_{\mathrm{cf}}=\sum_{k=2,4,6q=-k}^{k}B_{k}^{q}\hat{O}_{k}^{q}
$$
where $B_{k}^{q}$ are the crystal-field parameters and $\hat{o}_{k}^{q}$ are the operator equivalents of the crystal field potential and can be expressed as polynomials of the total momentum matrices, ${\cal J}^{2},{\cal J}_{z},{\cal J}_{-}$ and $J_{+}$ . The energy levels can be obtained by diagonalizing the crystalfield matrix. A general matrix element can be written as
$$
\langle J,m_{J}|\hat{H}_{\mathrm{cf}}|J^{\prime},m_{J}^{\prime}\rangle
$$
The $q\:=\:0$ terms are most important in determining the energy order of the $|m_{J}\rangle$ states, while the $q\ne0$ terms can dramatically shift the direction of the anisotropy axis to a direction that is far from the assumed axial position and are also responsible for generating an avoided level crossing through the couplings of the $|m_{J}\rangle$ and $|m_{J}\pm q\rangle$ basis wave functions that account for the under-barrier processes.15
The magnetization dynamics of lanthanide SMMs can occur via multiple spin–lattice relaxation pathways, including the Orbach, Raman and direct processes, as well as quantum tunneling of the magnetization, QTM. Different pathways may dominate in different temperature ranges in one system.16 The Orbach process behaves as a thermally activated two-phonon process and is associated with the real energy levels. The Raman process also behaves as a two-phonon process but proceeds via a virtual state. The direct process is a one-phonon process that happens between two states directly. On the other hand, quantum tunneling between two energy-degenerate states makes the system revert to thermal equilibrium without energy exchange. The direct process and QTM always contribute a negative effect to the effective energy barrier.
Apart from the energy levels, another important factor that influences the magnetization dynamics is the relaxation rate between different energy levels. For example, zero-field QTM is an intrinsic property of lanthanide SIMs; however, if the relaxation rate of QTM is slow, a hysteresis loop with a coercive field of lanthanide SIMs can also be observed.
An ideal example of lanthanide SMMs with a high energy barrier was proposed by Ungur and Chibotaru to illustrate the aforementioned theoretical model. In an ideal axial symmetric complex, $\mathrm{[DyO]^{+}}$ , all its electronic states exhibit anisotropy axes parallel to the molecular axis and only the highest excited doublets, $m_{J}=\pm1/2$ , show transverse anisotropy $(g_{\perp}~\neq~0)$ .17 In this case, the relaxation occurs in the sixth excited state and the height of the energy barrier for magnetization reversal is higher than $3000~\mathrm{~K~}$ However, if there is a significant noncoincidence between different Kramers doublets, an Orbach process occurring via a single common state (for example, $\left|-15/2\right\rangle\ \rightarrow\ \left|-13/2\right\rangle\ \rightarrow\ \left|+15/2\right\rangle\right)$ will be enhanced and the relaxation tends to proceed via the first excited Kramers doublets.18
On the other hand, a transversal (non-axial) component of the crystal field will induce QTM. This phenomenon is usually observed in molecular systems in which fundamental particles transfer from one potential well to another without climbing over the energy barrier. QTM derives from the wave nature of particles, which is important for very tiny objects, and is realized through overlapping of the wave functions.19 However, zero-field
QTM usually results in the loss of remanent magnetization and a decrease in the effective energy barrier, which is not favorable for the utilization of molecular nanomagnets for high-density information storage.
The probability of observing QTM depends on the interaction of the wave functions. Once two wave functions overlap with each other, there will be a chance for the quantum tunneling mechanism to participate in the magnetization relaxation process and inevitably undermine the effective energy barriers, in contrast to what is predicted from theory.
QTM is widely observed in lanthanide SMMs due to the following factors: (a) a dipolar interaction that can lead to the mixing of $m_{J}$ sublevels; (b) crystal field distortion, which gives rise to transverse anisotropies; (c) a hyperfine interaction, which reveals the relation between the nucleus moment and the extranuclear magnetic field. It is worth noting that QTM is forbidden in Kramers’ systems as their degenerate doublets are orthogonal, meaning that they never overlap in theory. However, in realistic Kramers’ systems, QTM can occur due to the reasons discussed above.
To achieve enhanced dynamic magnetization behavior in lanthanide SMMs, QTM, as well as other relaxation processes, should be rationally controlled to fine-tune the effective energy barriers and the relaxation rate. A rational design strategy aims to avoid the contributions of the non-axial crystal-field components and the dipole–dipole interactions between lanthanide ions. Combining crystal-field theory and group theory, the non-diagonal matrix elements corresponding to the non-axial contributions of the crystal field are all zero in certain coordination geometries, such as $D_{4\mathrm{d}},D_{5\mathrm{h}},D_{6\mathrm{d}},S_{8},C_{\infty\mathrm{v}}$ and $D_{\infty\mathrm{h}}$ .20 That is to say, only the axial terms $(q\=0,\ k=2,\ 4,\ 6)$ of the crystal-field components remain, resulting in mutual orthogonal $m_{J}$ doublets and precluding the origin of QTM. In addition, both magnetic exchange and dipolar interactions have been shown to influence QTM, which is thus also an important consideration for structural design.
## 2.2 Survey of lanthanide SMMs with high energy barriers and specific symmetries
Controlling the coordination geometries of lanthanide ions is challenging because of their large ionic radius, high coordination numbers and flexible coordination environment. The coordination symmetry we discuss in this review is not strictly as ideal as in mathematical theory because distortion and deviation are always observed in real systems but in a rational approximation it can provide useful information. Discrete SMMs are not the main topic of this review; however, a survey of discrete lanthanide SMMs with selected examples will highlight the importance of symmetry factors in governing the magnetization dynamics and the height of the energy barriers.
2.2.1 Discrete systems. In 2003, Ishikawa et al. described SMM behavior in the phthalocyanine complexes $\left(\mathbf{Bu}_{4}\mathbf{N}\right)[\mathbf{Ln}(\mathbf{Pc})_{2}]$ (1 $,\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Tb}$ , Dy), which marked the start of the lanthanide SMM era.21 This series of complexes have a double-decker or sandwich structure. Each Pc provides four nitrogen donors to bond with the lanthanide center; the lanthanide ion lies in a $D_{4\mathrm{d}}^{}$ -symmetry, square antiprismatic coordination environment, resulting in a perfect axial ligand field. Ac susceptibilities of a diluted sample of $\left(\mathbf{Bu}_{4}\mathbf{N}\right)[\mathbf{Tb}(\mathbf{Pc})_{2}]$ in its yttrium analogue were measured at zero dc (direct current) field. The data below $50\mathrm{~K~}$ for the diluted sample was fitted to the Arrhenius law to give a remarkable effective energy barrier of $230~\mathrm{{\cm}}^{-1}$ (374 K) with a preexponential factor $\left(\tau_{0}\right)$ of $6.3\times10^{-8}\:\mathrm{s}.$ . Detailed studies showed that the magnitude of the effective energy barrier is in good agreement with the energy separation between the ground doublets, $|m_{J}|=6$ , and the first excited states, $|m_{J}|=5$ . The magnetization dynamics at higher temperatures can be assigned to the Orbach process, while at lower temperatures they can be accounted for by QTM and a Raman process.
Subsequently, the series has been diversified through redox or tailoring the Pc ligands: $[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{Pc}^{\prime})_{2}]^{n}$ can be an anionic, cationic or neutral complex and the ligands can be the parent phthalocyaninate ligand or a substituted analogue. Particularly, the heteroleptic compound, $[\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{Pc})(\mathrm{Pc}^{\prime})]$ (2), which contains one octa(tert-butylphenoxy)-substituted Pc ring and one bare Pc ring, exhibits the highest effective energy barrier $\left(652~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right.$ or 939 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.09\times{{10}^{-11}}$ s for a SMM. $^6\mathbf{A}$ butterfly-shaped hysteresis loop was observed at $^2\mathrm{~K~}$ for 2. The success of this double-decker $\left[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathbf{Pc})_{2}\right]^{n}$ family arises from their highly symmetric axial crystal fields. For comparison, deviation from ideal $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ symmetry results in a decrease in the effective energy barrier, as in $\left[\mathrm{Dy}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ (3) with a distorted $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ coordination polyhedron $\{\mathrm{DyO_{8}}\}$ .22 An Arrhenius fit of the relaxation time above $8\mathrm{~K~}$ gives an effective energy barrier of $45.9\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (66.1 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $8.0\times10^{-7}\ s$ . Moreover, a butterfly-shaped hysteresis loop can be observed at $0.5\mathrm{~K~}$ for 3.
Planar cyclooctatetraene (COT) systems are another family of lanthanide SMMs with rotational symmetry. The structure of $\left[\mathrm{Er}(\mathrm{COT})_{2}\right]^{-}$ (4) is composed of two $\mathrm{COT}^{2-}$ ligands bound in an $\boldsymbol{\upeta}^{8}$ fashion to the central $\mathrm{Er}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ ion. The $\mathrm{COT}_{\mathrm{centroid}^{-}}\mathrm{Er}^{\mathrm{III}}-$ $\mathbf{COT_{\mathrm{{centroid}}}}$ angle of $178.04^{\circ}$ reflects the almost perfect linearity of the whole molecule (Fig. 1).23 Compound 4 shows an axial symmetry very close to $D_{\mathrm{8d}}$ and the ac susceptibility signals of 4 can be detected under zero dc field, exhibiting very strong blocking of magnetization with an effective energy barrier of $198~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (286 K) and a $\tau_{0}$ of $3.7\times10^{-9}\:s$ . Compound 4 shows hysteresis loops with large coercive fields, achieving 7000 Oe at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ , and the hysteresis loops can persist to $12\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 1).
The $D_{5\mathrm{h}}$ symmetry of the lanthanide ion is observed in two interesting trinuclear complexes $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{Dy(L^{1})}_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH)}]\mathrm{NO}_{3}{\cdot}3\mathrm{MeOH}.$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (5, ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf L}^{1}=2,2^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime}$ -(((nitrilotris(ethane-2,1-diyl))tris(azanediyl))- tris(methylene))tris-(4-bromophenol)24 and $\mathrm{[Fe_{2}D y(L^{2})_{2}(H_{2}O)]C l O_{4}}.$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (6, ${\bf H}_{3}{\bf L}^{2}=2,2^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime}$ -(((nitrilotris(ethane-2,1-diyl))tris(azanediyl))- tris(methylene))tris-(4-chlorophenol)),25 where the central DyIII ion exhibits pentagonal-bipyramidal coordination geometry in both complexes (Fig. 2a). The well-designed tripodal ligand successfully provides a four-nitrogen cavity for the transition metal ions and three outer oxygen donors for the central DyIII ion. Particularly, the coordinated solvents constitute the remaining oxygen site of the pentagonal plane, playing an essential role in determining the high axial symmetries and hence enhancing SMM behavior. Compound 5 loses its coordinated methanol molecule after being exposed to dry air for one day, giving rise to a new composition of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{Dy}(\mathrm{L}^{1})_{2}]\mathrm{NO}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (7). Ac susceptibility measurements of 5 and 7 were carried out at zero dc field. A strong frequency dependence of the out-of-phase signals of 5 are observed below $30\mathrm{~K~}$ . The fit to the Arrhenius law gives an effective energy barrier of $305\pm3~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(439\pm5\mathrm{~K~})$ and a relaxation time $\tau$ of 0.36 s (2 K) (Fig. 2b). The relaxation time increases to 6000 s at low temperatures under an external field of $1000\mathrm{Oe}$ . Compound 7 does not show a peak of out-of-phase signals at zero dc field, due to fast QTM. When applying an optimized 1200 Oe field, peaks of out-of-phase signals of 7 are observed below 15.0 K $\left(1488~\mathrm{Hz}\right)$ with an effective energy barrier of $44\pm2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(64\pm2)$ and a $\tau$ of 0.16 s (3.5 K). Structural and magnetic characterization of 5 and 7 reveals that the coordination symmetries of the $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions change from $D_{5\mathrm{h}}$ to $O_{\mathrm{h\ell}}$ , while the energy barriers change from $305~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to a negligible value. The observed hysteresis loops are butterfly-shaped, which is typical for SIMs because QTM at zero field leads to loss of magnetization (Fig. 2c).
In 6, the very high ac peak temperatures and strong frequency dependence of the ac susceptibilities under zero dc field suggest a thermally activated slow magnetic relaxation process with an effective energy barrier of $319~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (459 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.11\times$ ${10}^{\ensuremath{-}10}$ s. The paramagnetic $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are ferromagnetically coupled with the $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion and result in a Kramers system, which supports a doubly degenerate ground state. The ferromagnetic coupling together with the $D_{5\mathrm{h}}$ symmetry of the DyIII ion is favorable for the SMM behavior, leading to a record anisotropy barrier for 3d–4f SMMs. No magnetization hysteresis loops were reported for 6.
Theoretically, a linear two-coordinate complex would create the simplest but strongest axial ligand environment where the donor atoms are exclusively located on a single Cartesian axis. However, this is a great challenge in terms of synthesis. Indeed it is difficult for lanthanide complexes to achieve very low coordination numbers (CNs) because of their large ionic radius. $\left[\mathrm{Sm}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{Si}^{\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{Pr}_{3})_{2})_{2}\right]$ (8) has been reported as the first and only near-linear two-coordinate bis(amide) f-element complex for SMM study.26 The central $\mathrm{Sm}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ion is bivalent and is bonded by two sterically demanding ligands with an $\scriptstyle\mathbf{N}-\mathbf{S}\mathbf{m}-\mathbf{N}$ angle of $175.52^{\circ}$ (Fig. 3). Although this $\mathrm{Sm}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complex does not show
SMM behavior, ab initio calculation predicts that its DyIII analogue would achieve magnetization dynamics via the highest energy doublets (the 7th excited states) and possess an effective energy barrier of $1800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (2500 K), which is inspiring and eagerly anticipated by synthetic chemists.
2.2.2 High-dimensional systems. As defined previously, MOFs are 3D coordination polymer frameworks and are crystalline materials constructed from inorganic building units and organic linkers. Once these inorganic and organic building units (often termed as secondary building units, SBUs) are assembled, ordered framework structures with well-defined structural topologies can be obtained, for which the magnetic properties originating from the inorganic SBUs will be discussed in the following section. A synthetic strategy using the concept of SBUs was proposed by Yaghi and co-workers as ‘‘reticular’’ (network) chemistry.27 On the basis of the explosive growth of MOFs, the synthesis of new structures requires not only chemical skill but also knowledge of the topological principles.
A motivation for arranging lanthanide SMMs into MOFs is that the lanthanide ion serves as an inorganic SBU, in which the coordination sphere can be constrained by the surrounding organic linkers. In other words, through rational design, the coordination geometries of lanthanide ions can be fixed in framework structures and, if the coordination symmetry is ‘‘right’’, SMM behavior can be observed.
In general, 2-fold, 3-fold, 4-fold, 6-fold and 8-fold rotation axes can be perfectly achieved in framework structures because of the symmetry operation requirements of the space groups in crystalline materials. Due to the recent rapid development of MOF chemistry, there is a large database containing magnetically interesting framework structures.
In addition, it is possible to achieve low CNs for lanthanide ions in framework structures. In general, CNs of lanthanide complexes range from eight to twelve due to the large ionic radii. It is still quite challenging to synthesize lanthanide complexes with low coordination numbers, even using bulky ligands to prevent further coordination in order to satisfy the coordination requirements. Fortunately, the framework structure of MOFs is beneficial with respect to fixing the lanthanide ion in a given coordination environment and thus providing a new platform for low CN lanthanide complexes.28
In the following section, most of the examples are MOFs with 3D framework structures constructed via coordination bonds; however, several excellent examples with 2D framework structures are also included. The detailed structural dimensionality resulting from coordination bonds can be found in Table 1.
A Dy-MOF $\{\mathrm{[Dy(bipyNO)_{4}](T f O)_{3}}\}_{n}$ (9, bipyNO $\mathbf{\Sigma}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bypyridyl$N,N^{\prime}$ -dioxide, TfO $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ triflate) has been successfully obtained, in which the dysprosium center has a square-antiprismatic coordination geometry.29 Each $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion is coordinated by eight oxygen atoms from eight individual dioxide linkers and shows a quasi- $\cdot D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ symmetry. The bipyNO ligands act as bridges between neighboring DyIII ions with a Dy $\cdot\cdot$ Dy distance of $13{\mathring{\mathbf{A}}}.$ . In this case, $\mathrm{{Dy}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}$ ions are well isolated by diamagnetic organic components. Magnetic measurements confirm the presence of magnetization dynamics. Due to fast QTM, no maximum of the out-of-phase signals is observed at zero external dc field. In an external field of $1000\mathrm{Oe}$ , QTM is suppressed and the energy barrier can be obtained as $17.9\mathrm{~K~}$ with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.86\times{{10}^{-7}}{\mathrm{~s~}}$ . The three crystallographically independent DyIII ions in the structures are responsible for the relatively low energy barrier because of the different orientations of their anisotropic axes.
Lanthanide ions with square-antiprismatic $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ geometry can also be obtained in heterometallic MOFs. $[\mathrm{Dy}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{d}_{2}(\mathrm{PIDC}).$ - $\mathrm{(HPIDC)}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{n}$ (10, $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{PIDC}=2\mathbf{\cdot}$ -(pyridine-4-yl)-1H-imidazole4,5-dicarboxylic acid) possesses eight-coordinate $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites with distorted $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ coordination geometry. $^{30}\mathrm{\Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions are connected through diamagnetic $\mathbf{Cd}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions and organic ligands into a framework. In zero external dc field, no maximum is observed in the dynamic magnetization due to fast QTM. Enhanced magnetic properties with a $U_{\mathrm{eff}}$ of $55\mathrm{~K~}$ and a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.31\times{10}^{-8}$ s was obtained by application of $\mathbf{Y}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ -dilution and an external field of $600\mathrm{\:{Oe}}$ .
$\mathrm{\small{[Dy(3-py-4-pmc)(C_{2}O_{4})_{0.5}(O H)(H_{2}O)]}}_{n}$ (11, $3\mathrm{-py-4-pmc}=2\mathrm{-}($ (3- pyridyl)pyrimidine-4-carboxyl) provides an example in which both single-ion anisotropy and magnetic interactions contribute to the magnetization dynamics to give non-zero coercive fields in the hysteresis loops.31 Compound 11 contains square-antiprismatic $\{\mathrm{DyO_{8}}\}$ coordination polyhedra, which are connected by carboxylate, oxalate and hydroxide bridges. Each $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion in 11 is eightcoordinated by two carboxylate oxygen atoms from two 3-py-4- pmc ligands, three oxalate atoms, two hydroxyl groups and one water molecule (Fig. 4a and b). The carboxylate groups bridge the $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions to give a chain along the $b$ direction. The chains are further connected by the oxalate groups to form a layer (Fig. 4c). Ac susceptibilities are strongly frequency-dependent below $40\mathrm{K}$ under zero dc field, giving an effective energy barrier of $186\mathrm{K}$ with a $\tau_{0}$ of $2.44\times{{10}^{-8}}$ s (Fig. 4d). Moreover, butterflyshaped hysteresis loops are observed below $5\mathrm{~K~}$ at a sweep rate of 500 Oe $\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4e). The magnetic behavior of 11 can be ascribed to the single-ion behavior of the DyIII ion with $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ symmetry and the magnetic coupling derived from the strong interaction between $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions via hydroxide groups can efficiently suppress QTM, leading to pronounced hysteresis loops (Fig. 4e).
$\{({\bf H}_{3}{\bf O})[{\bf D y}({\bf N A})_{2}]{\cdot}{\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}\}_{n}$ (12, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{NA}=5$ -hydroxynicotinic acid) shows an unusual coordination chemistry in that the central DyIII ion is surrounded by six oxygen atoms from two hydroxy groups and four carboxylate groups of six separate $\mathrm{NA}^{2-}$ ligands (Fig. 5).28 Such an unusually low CN was achieved with the help of the framework structure. The six-coordinate $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions are connected by $\upmu_{1,3}$ carboxylate groups to form infinite chains, which are further linked by the ligands into a layer. Ac susceptibilities were measured below $20\mathrm{~K~}$ under zero dc field; however, out-ofphase peaks were not observed, mainly due to QTM. A magneticsite-diluted sample of 12 using diamagnetic $\mathbf{Y}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ ions was also synthesized. Ac susceptibilities of diluted 12 showed frequencydependent in-phase and out-of-phase signals under zero dc field, giving an effective energy barrier of $75\mathrm{K}$ with a $\tau_{0}$ of $4.21\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{s}.$ In addition, butterfly-shaped hysteresis loops for the diluted sample were clearly observed from 2 to $5\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 5).
Most of the aforementioned examples have $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ symmetry with respect to the lanthanide center, except for the one with a low CN of six. Eight-coordinate square antiprismatic geometry is common for the later lanthanide ions. Obtaining other coordination symmetries of lanthanide ions in MOFs requires rational design of the bridging ligands. For example, a $C_{5}$ axis is not available in crystalline materials due to symmetry operation requirements; however, this could be achieved in quasi-crystals. A possible way to obtain coordination symmetry with a $C_{5}$ axis is to dope lanthanide ions, such as $\mathrm{{Dy}^{\mathrm{{III}}}/\mathrm{{Tb}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}}$ ions, into a diamagnetic quasi-crystal, in which the original $C_{5}$ positions are occupied by tripositive metal ions.
## 3. Assembly of MBBs in framework structures
Coordination chemistry has shown great potential in the synthesis of magnetic MOFs based on versatile MBBs (Table 2). It is noted that some MBBs are not available as discrete systems due to the laws of chemical thermodynamics. Due to the assembly process driven by coordination bonding, some unusual MBBs can be stabilized by the strength of the frameworks and this process also results in a lattice energy that can lower the total energy of the system.
SMMs/SCMs are magnetically anisotropic molecular species. With regard to practical applications, assembly of SMMs/SCMs with their anisotropic axes pointing in a specified direction is important. This situation is much easier to achieve in frameworks than in discrete molecules because the structural building units must lie in positions that are symmetry-allowed.
MOFs can also provide a new platform for fine-tuning the magnetic properties of MBBs assembled into frameworks though the following strategies: (i) isostructural MOFs can be designed and synthesized by varying the inorganic nodes and organic linkers individually or simultaneously, to provide excellent systems to study the influence of electronic structure and magnetic interaction pathways on the magnetization dynamics; (ii) the microporous structure can accommodate different guest molecules. Exchange of the guest molecules can cause subtle changes in the MBBs, which will lead to different magnetic properties; (iii) some flexible MOFs can also show diverse magnetic properties upon external stimuli. This interesting phenomenon has been highlighted by Coronado and Espallargas in a recent review and thus will not be covered here.12
## 3.1 Diverse MBBs in framework structures
It is quite challenging to rationally control the arrangement of MBBs and the magnetic interactions between them. The magnetic properties of molecular magnets strongly depend on the assembly of MBBs. By adjusting the linkers, different types of molecular magnets can be achieved in high-dimensional frameworks by arranging the MBBs with the help of topological design.
The frameworks can consist of single metal ions, coordination clusters and coordination chains as MBBs.
3.1.1 Transition-metal MBBs in MOFs. High-dimensional coordination frameworks can show magnetization dynamics originating from single metal centers or coordination clusters connected by bulky diamagnetic organic ligands. These magnetic interactions, including exchange couplings and dipole–dipole interactions, are weak and can be neglected, thus the metal ions/clusters serving as both nodes and MBBs in the frameworks are well isolated magnetically, especially in the case of lanthanide MOFs. In these circumstances, the SMM behavior that might be observed essentially depends on the intrinsic features of the metal nodes.
In $\{[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{azbbpy})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})_{0.5}(\mathrm{DMF})(\mathrm{NCS})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{MeOH}\}_{n}$ (13, azbbpy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,3-bis(4-pyridyl)azulene, $4,4^{\prime}–\mathrm{bipy~=~4},4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine, DMF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dimethylformamide),32 each $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ion acts as a six-connected node coordinated by two nitrogen atoms from the pyridyl fragments, two nitrogen atoms from the thiocyanato terminal ligands in transpositions, one nitrogen atom from the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine bridge and one oxygen atom from a DMF molecule, forming a slightly distorted octahedral coordination environment. Each cobalt center is connected to three neighboring nodes by two azbbpy and one $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy, to construct a hexagonal mesh. The shortest distance between $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions is $11.48{\hat{\mathrm{~\bf~A~}}},$ , which means there will be no significant magnetic exchange coupling between them. Ac susceptibility measurement under zero dc field shows no out-ofphase signals, due to fast QTM. The out-of-phase ac susceptibilities of 13 can be turned on under a $1000\mathrm{Oe}$ dc field, to give an effective energy barrier of $9.7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (14 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.2\times{{10}^{-6}}$ s. No hysteresis loop was observed for 13.
$\{[\mathrm{CO}_{7}(\mathrm{TPA})_{6}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\upmu_{2}-\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\cdot2\mathrm{TPA}\cdot3\mathrm{DMF}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ (14, $\mathbf{HTPA}=4$ -(1,2,4-triazol-4-ly)phenylacetic acid) exhibits a 6-connected 3D framework based on heptanuclear $\mathbf{Co}_{7}$ clusters.33 The central $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ion is linked to the other six $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions via four $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu_{3}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}^{-}$ groups to form a $\mathbf{Co}_{7}$ core, which is further stabilized by two $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu_{2}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ , four $\upmu_{1,3}$ -carboxylate groups and six $\upmu_{1,2}$ -triazole groups. All the $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions are six-coordinate, showing distorted octahedral geometry. Each $\mathbf{Co}_{7}$ cluster is connected to six adjacent $\mathbf{Co}_{7}$ units through twelve $\mathrm{TPA}^{-}$ ligands in a double-walled model. Ac susceptibility measurement was carried out at zero dc field.
Frequency dependence of the ac signals is observed below $5\mathrm{~K~}$ but no out-of-phase peaks are found above $2\mathrm{K}$ , as a result of fast QTM. By applying an optimized external field of $1800~\mathrm{Oe}$ , the effective energy barrier was obtained as $23.6~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (34 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $2.2\times10^{-8}$ s. No hysteresis loop was observed.
$\{[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{19}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{a}(\upmu_{4}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O})_{9}(\upmu_{3}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O})(\upmu_{3}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{H})_{3}(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e})_{9}(\mathrm{pd})_{9}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{3}]\mathbf{O}\mathbf{H}\cdot0.5-0\}_{\mathbf{N}_{1}\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{N}_{3}}\}_{\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{N}_{3}\mathbf{N}_{3}}.$ $\mathbf{MeCN}{\cdot}6\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\}_{n}$ (15, $\mathbf{H}_{2}{\mathsf{p d}}=1{,}3$ -propanediol) has an open framework structure based on the $\bf{M n}_{19}$ clusters (Fig. 6).34 The $\bf{M n}_{19}$ core consists of three equilateral triangles stacked one above the other. The basal triangle contains ten manganese ions, of which the central one is surrounded by nine others on the edges. The middle and apex triangles contain six and three manganese ions, respectively. The metal centers are held together by $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ groups, to give a triangular-pyramidal-frustum geometry, where a frustum is defined as the solid formed from two parallel planes cutting a pyramidal or conical structure. The $\bf{M n}_{19}$ aggregates are connected into a 3D framework through $\upmu_{3}{\bf-O}_{2}{\bf C M e}$ ligands and $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions. Static magnetic measurements reveal that 15 possesses a ground state spin $S=23/2$ and a zero-field splitting parameter $D=-0.086(3)\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The frequency dependence of the ac susceptibilities can be detected below $2.8\mathrm{~K~}$ without peaks, which is indicative of the presence of magnetization dynamics. The SMM behavior is further confirmed by hysteresis loops observed below $1.1\mathrm{~K~}$ using a micro-SQUID apparatus (Fig. 6).
3.1.2 Lanthanide MBBs in MOFs. Binuclear discrete MBB $\mathrm{\small{[Dy_{2}(h m i)_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]}}$ (16, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{h}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{i}=$ (isonicotino)hydrazine) can be assembled into a network of $\mathrm{\small{[Dy_{2}(h m i)_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]}}_{n}.$ MeCN (17). Compound 16 has a $\{\mathrm{Dy}_{2}\}$ core, in which the metal centers are bridged by phenoxide groups from two hmi ligands (Fig. 7).35 The DyIII center is eight-coordinated by one nitrogen atom and four oxygen atoms from two hmi ligands, two oxygen atoms from a chelating ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ anion and one methanol molecule. Compound 17 features similar $\{\mathrm{Dy}_{2}\}$ units except that the pyridyl nitrogen atoms further coordinate to $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions to form a framework (Fig. 7). In 17, the DyIII ion is nine-coordinate. Both 16 and 17 show magnetization dynamics. The well scattered frequency dependence of the ac susceptibilities can be obtained at optimized external dc fields of $800\ \mathrm{Oe}$ or $700\ \mathrm{{Oe}}$ , to give effective energy barriers of $38.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (56 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $3\times10^{-7}\mathrm{{s}}$ and $49~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (71 K) with $\tau_{0}$ of $7\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~s~}$ for 16 and 17, respectively (Fig. 7).
The organization of the binuclear MBB in the framework significantly increases the energy barrier for magnetization reversal. No hysteresis loop was reported.
$\{\mathrm{[Na_{4}D y_{12}(s t p)_{8}(O H)_{16}(H_{2}O)_{12}]{\cdot}10H_{2}O\}_{n}}$ (18, $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfstp}=2$ -sulfoterephthalic acid) possesses a $\left\{\mathrm{Ln}_{12}\big(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}\big)_{16}\right\}^{20+}$ wheel-cluster core, which is rather rare in MOFs.36 The dodecanuclear core is based on four vertex-sharing cubane-like $\left\{\mathrm{Dy}_{4}\big(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}\big)_{4}\right\}^{8+}$ units, in which all $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions are in a distorted eight-coordinate square antiprism geometry. The neighboring $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}\mathbf{y}_{12}$ clusters are linked by pairs of $\scriptstyle\upmu_{2}-\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ to form a chain along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ direction. The $\mathsf{s t p}^{3-}$ ligands serve as $\upmu_{5}$ -bridges and connect the chains into a 3D framework (Fig. 8). Ac susceptibility measurement was carried out under zero dc field, to give an effective energy barrier of $9.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (13.7 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $8.82~\times~10^{-7}$ s. No hysteresis loop was reported for 18.
3.1.3 Mixed transition-lanthanide MBBs in MOFs. A series of 3d–4f heterometallic MOFs are successfully synthesized with $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{BPDC}\right)$ . $\mathrm{\big[DyCu\big(BPDC\big)_{2}.}$ - $\left({\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}\right)_{2}{\bf C l}]_{n}$ (19) contains one eight-coordinate $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion with a distorted dicapped trigonal prism coordination geometry and one five-coordinate square pyramidal $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ion.37 The $\mathbf{BPDC}^{2-}$ anions connect DyIII and $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ions into a 3D framework with channels along the $b$ direction. Ac susceptibilities are measured at zero dc field, however, no out-of-phase peaks are observed due to QTM. When a dc field of 2000 Oe is applied to suppress QTM, an energy barrier of $29.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (42.4 K) with $\tau_{0}$ of $3.43\ \times$ ${{10}^{-9}}$ s is yielded from the best fit to the Arrhenius law. No hysteresis loop was observed for 19.
$\{\mathrm{[Dy_{2}C u(B P D C)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{4}](N O_{3})_{2}}\}_{n}~(2$ $\left(2\mathbf{0}\right)^{37}$ and $\{\mathrm{[Dy_{2}Z n(B P D C)_{3}-}}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)_{4}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}\}_{n}\left(2\mathbf{1}\right)^{38}$ possess the same cationic 3D frameworks featuring binuclear $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{y}}_{2}$ units with different counter ions. The DyIII ions exhibit distorted tricapped trigonal prism geometry and are connected into a binuclear $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{y}}_{2}$ node by both carboxylate oxygen atoms and carboxylate groups. Ac susceptibilities of 20 and 21 are measured under zero dc field; however, no clear out-of-phase peaks are observed due to QTM. In order to suppress QTM, external 2000 Oe and 5000 Oe dc fields are applied for 20 and 1000 Oe and 5000 Oe fields for 21. The effective energy barrier of $66.3~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (95.3 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $0.94\times$ ${{10}^{-12}}$ s for 20 and $63.2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (90.9 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $3.84\times10^{-10}\mathrm{~s~}$ for 21 were obtained using the ac data at 5000 Oe. No hysteresis loops were reported for 20 or 21.
3.1.4 Encapsulation of SMMs in the pores of MOFs. Another synthetic strategy to obtain SMMs in framework structures is to introduce SMM molecules into the pores of MOFs to take advantage of their porous nature. This synthetic challenge was recently realized and reported in the literature.
The prototypical SMM molecule $\mathrm{\small{[Mn_{12}O_{12}(O_{2}C M e)_{16}(H_{2}O)_{4}]}}$ $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{12}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{c}\right)$ was successfully introduced as a guest into the mesoporous aluminum-based MOF $[\mathrm{Al(OH)}(\mathrm{SDC)}]_{n}$ (CYCU-3, $\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}\mathrm{\bfSDC}=4,4^{\prime}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid) to form $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{12}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{c}(\overline{{\mathbf{a}}})}\end{array}$ CYCU-3 (22).39 CYCU-3 consists of hexagonal channels of about $3\:\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter, which are large enough to accommodate the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{12}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{c}$ molecules whose shape can be described as a $1.1\ \times$ $1.6\:\mathrm{nm}$ discoid (Fig. 9). The ac susceptibility data were obtained under zero dc field. Clear out-of-phase ac susceptibility signals for $\mathbf{Mn_{12}A c}$ were observed with peaks in the range $3.4\substack{-5.8\mathrm{~K~}}$ indicating an effective energy barrier of $48.7\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (70 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.2\times{{10}^{-8}}{{\mathrm{s}}}$ . The magnetization dynamics of 22 are similar to those of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ , which possesses an effective energy barrier of $39.6~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (57 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $5.2\times{10}^{-9}{\mathrm{~s~}}$ . Compound 22 shows an obvious hysteresis loop with a coercive field of $2000\mathrm{\Omega}{\mathrm{Oe}}$ . Meanwhile, the incorporated SMMs exhibit a significantly enhanced thermal stability originating from the robust framework.
An SMM moiety $\mathrm{\small{[Mn^{III}(s a l e n)(H_{2}O)]_{2}}^{2+}}$ $\left(\mathrm{salen}^{2-}\ =\ N,N^{\prime}\right.$ - ethylenebis-(salicylideneiminate)) has been successfully inserted into a ferromagnetic bimetallic oxalate network to afford a hybrid MOF of $\{\mathrm{[Mn^{III}(s a l e n)(H_{2}O)]_{2}[M n^{I I}C r^{I I I}(o x)_{3}]_{2}}$ MeOH 2MeCN} (23).40 The cationic $\left\{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\right\}$ MBB is composed of two $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ ions bridged by two $\upmu$ -phenolate oxygen atoms (Fig. 10). The coordination environment of each metal center is completed by $\left\{{\bf N}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}\right\}$ equatorial atoms, one water molecule and one phenolate oxygen atom from the neighboring $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ MBB (Fig. 10). For comparison, two analogous MOFs, $\{[\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{salen})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]_{2}\cdot\mathbf{M}$ $\mathbf{MeOH}{\cdot}2\mathbf{MeCN}\}$ (24) and $\{[\mathrm{In^{III}(s a l_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}t r i e n)}][\mathrm{Mn^{II}C r^{I I I}(o x)}_{3}]\cdot0.25\mathrm{H_{2}O\cdot0.25\mathrm{MeOH\cdot0.25\mathrm{MeCN}}}\}$ (25) were prepared as reference compounds for the SMM behavior of $\mathrm{\small{[Mn^{III}(s a l e n)(H_{2}O)]_{2}}^{2+}}$ in the lattice and the ferromagnetic behavior of the $[\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{H}}\mathbf{C}\mathrm{r}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathbf{ox})_{3}]$ lattice, respectively. Compound 24 exhibits SMM behavior in the absence of the ferromagnetic lattice due to substitution by $\mathrm{Zn^{II}}$ ions. Ac susceptibilities of 24 measured in the presence of various dc magnetic fields show well-defined temperature dependence behavior under an optimized external field of $0.5\mathrm{~T~}$ and the effective energy barrier is $20.8\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (30 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $2\times{10}^{-10}\:\mathrm{s}.$ In 25, ferromagnetism in the bimetallic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ lattice with the diamagnetic $\left\{\mathbf{In}_{2}\right\}$ unit is observed at $T_{\mathrm{c}}=5\mathrm{~K~}$ . This work showed that insertion of the SMM in the ferromagnetic lattice gives a larger coercive field for the hysteresis loop of 23 than that of 24 and an effective energy barrier of $15.3~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (22 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $7\times10^{-9}$ s, which results from combination of the SMM molecules with the ferromagnetic lattice. No clear hysteresis loops were observed for 24 and 25.
3.1.5 One-dimensional transition-metal coordination chains in MOFs. All the examples in this section are $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ chains assembled into 2D or 3D framework structures. Ferromagnetic interactions are generally observed. Strong intrachain magnetic couplings between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions contribute significantly to the correlation energy $(\varDelta_{\5})$ that represents an additional contribution to the energy barrier for magnetization reversal. These chains are difficult to isolate without the frameworks.
[CoII(trans-1,2-chdc)] (26, trans-1,2-chdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ trans-1,2-cyclohexanedicarboxylate) consists of a parallel arrangement of carboxylate-bridged, paddle-wheel $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ chains.41 The $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ atom possesses a slightly distorted square-pyramidal geometry, bonding with four basal oxygen atoms and one apical oxygen atom. Two inversely related $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions are bridged by four $\upmu$ -carboxylate groups into a paddle-wheel dimer. The dimers are further bridged by two $\upmu{\cdot}$ -carboxylato-O bridges into a chain (Fig. 11). The chains are interlinked into layers by trans-1,2-chdc ligands with a shortest interchain $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ distance of $5.5\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$
Ferromagnetic interactions between $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ions can be revealed by dc magnetic susceptibility measurements. Ac susceptibility measurements were carried out at zero dc field. An Arrhenius plot extracted from the out-of-phase peaks shows the occurrence of a clear crossover between two different activated regions, to give a $\Delta\tau_{1}/k_{\mathrm{B}}$ of $56.3~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (80.9 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $5.19\times{{10}^{-11}}$ and a $\Delta\tau_{2}/k_{\mathrm{B}}$ of $34.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (50.2 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $5.59\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~s~}$ . This result is in agreement with the presence of structural defects along the chain, which limits the growth of the correlation. Hysteresis loops with orientation- and temperature-dependent shapes clearly appear below $2.2\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 11).
$\mathrm{\small{[Co_{2}(H_{0.67}b d t)_{3}]cdot20H_{2}O}}$ (27, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{dt}=5,5^{\prime}$ -(1,4-phenylene)bis(1H-tetrazole)) is a 3D framework constructed from {Co(tetrazolate)}n chains running parallel to the $a$ axis and linked through the phenyl tethers of the bdt ligands.42 Each $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ion is surrounded by six nitrogen donors from bdt ligands to form an octahedral $\{\mathrm{CoN}_{6}\}$ coordination geometry. Each chain is linked to four neighboring chains to generate rectangular channels with approximate dimensions of $12.0~\times~9.0~\mathrm{{\stackrel{\circ}{A}}},$ , occupied by $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules. Zero-field ac susceptibility measurements gave broad frequencydependent out-of-phase signals below $5\mathrm{K}$ and an activation energy of $30.2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (43.4 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $5.1\times{10}^{-9}$ s. In addition, a hysteresis loop was observed at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ with a coercive field of $450\mathrm{\mathrm{~Oe~}}$ , further confirming the SCM behavior. A dry sample of 27 without guest water molecules did not show an out-of-phase ac signal. Furthermore, the SCM properties of 27 could not be restored by resolvation of the dry sample owing to irreversible damage to the crystals on drying.
$\mathrm{{[{Co}_{5}(O H)_{2}(p t a)_{4}(b t x)_{2}]_{{n}}}}$ (28, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{p}\mathbf{t}\mathbf{a}=$ phthalic acid, $\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tx}=\mathbf{1},\mathbf{4}\mathbf{-}$ bis(1,2,4-triazole-1-ylmethyl)benzene) contains $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ chains and shows both magnetization dynamics and hysteresis loops with an antiferromagnetic ground state.43 There are three crystallographically independent ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions and all of them are located in distorted octahedral coordination environments. Five $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are held together by two $\mathsf{{\Pi}}\mathsf{{|}\Pi_{3}\mathrm{{-}\mathrm{{OH}^{-}}}}$ groups to form an unusual centrosymmetric pentanuclear $\{{\bf C o}_{5}\}$ moiety by sharing the vertex of two $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}.$ -triangles. The $\left\{{\bf C o}_{5}\right\}$ units are further linked by $\upmu-\upeta^{1}:\upeta^{1}$ oxygen atoms from $\mathsf{p t a}^{2-}$ ligands to give an infinite chain. Adjacent chains are connected through cis-btx ligands to form a network structure (Fig. 12). Plots of $\ln(\chi T)$ vs. $1/T$ with different dc fields were analyzed and showed linearity in the temperature range $6.5\substack{-22\mathrm{~K~}}$ , confirming the uniaxial anisotropy of the Ising-like chain. An energy of the correlation length, $\begin{array}{r}{A_{\xi}/k_{\mathrm{B}},}\end{array}$ of $38.1\mathrm{~K~}$ $(26.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ is obtained by fitting the data with the equation $\begin{array}{r l r}{\chi T}&{{}=}&{C_{\mathrm{eff}}\exp\left(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{\xi}}/k_{\mathrm{B}}T\right)}\end{array}$ . The antiferromagnetic ground state of 28 can be confirmed by the significant drop of $\ln(\chi T)$ at zero dc field below $5\mathrm{~K~}$ Ac magnetic susceptibilities reveal that 28 possesses two relaxation processes with energy barriers of $63.83~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (91.8 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.96\times{10}^{-10}$ s in a high temperature regime and $49.7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (71.4 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.06\times{{10}^{-8}}$ s in a low temperature regime. At $2.0~\mathrm{K},$ a hysteresis loop is observed with a coercive field of $90\ {\mathrm{Oe}}$ , indicative of SCM behavior.
$\{[\mathrm{CO}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{N}_{3})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\}_{n}$ (29, $\mathrm{~\:~HL~}=~1\cdot$ -carboxymethylpyridinium-3-carboxylic acid) contains cobalt chains within a framework and shows SCM behavior.44 The $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ chain is made up of centrosymmetric trinuclear building units linked by $\upmu$ -EO${\mathbf{N_{3}}}^{-}$ bridges. Dc magnetic susceptibility reveals that 29 exhibits ferromagnetic interactions between $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ions. The field-cooled and zero-field-cooled magnetizations measured at 20 Oe show divergence below $4.5~\mathrm{~K~}$ , indicative of magnet-like behavior. Compound 29 shows the presence of magnetization relaxation with an effective energy barrier of $28.5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (41.0 K) and a $\tau_{0}$ of $8.1\times10^{-10}$ s obtained by ac susceptibility measurements under zero dc field. No hysteresis loop was mentioned for 29. Interestingly, the coordinated and guest water molecules in 29 can be removed by heating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the dehydrated phase shows antiferromagnetic coupled ordering at $5.5~\mathrm{K}.$ .
An unusual water-bridged homospin $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ chain has been successfully assembled in a MOF, $[\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mathbf{L})_{2}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathbf{DMF})_{4}]_{n}$ (30, $\begin{array}{l l l}{{{\bf{H}}_{2}{\bf{L}}}}&{{=}}&{{3,3^{\prime}}}\end{array}$ -(1,3,6,8-tetraoxobenzol $[\mathrm{lmn}][3,8]$ -phenanthroline$2,7(1H,3H,6H,8H)\mathrm{diyl})$ -dibenzoic acid).45 The $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{II}}$ ion in 30 exhibits slightly compressed octahedral geometry. The adjacent $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{II}}$ ions are connected by water bridges to construct infinite chains that are further linked through $\scriptstyle\mathbf{L}^{2-}$ ligands to form a 3D framework. The obvious frequency dependence of ac susceptibilities under zero dc field shows a dual relaxation process. The Arrhenius fit gives an effective energy barrier of $15.1\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (21.6 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $7.81\times10^{-9}$ s. By applying the optimized 600 Oe dc field, a change from a dual relaxation to a single relaxation process can be obtained and the effective energy barrier is found to be $13.53\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (19.46 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.56\times10^{-8}\mathrm{s}$ . Analysis of the plots of $\ln(\chi T)$ vs. $1/T$ at different dc fields confirms Ising chain behavior with a $\varDelta_{\xi}/k_{\mathrm{B}}$ of $6.44\mathrm{K}$ , suggesting that the water-bridged chain within this MOF is a SCM. No clear hysteresis loop was observed at $2~\mathrm{K}.$
3.1.6 One-dimensional lanthanide coordination chains in MOFs. MOFs based on lanthanide coordination chains as MBBs showing magnetization dynamics is still rare. The magnetic behavior cannot be categorized as SCM because intrachain magnetic couplings between lanthanide ions are weak and the values of $\varDelta_{\xi}/k_{\mathrm{B}}$ that correspond to the correlation of the spin carriers are usually small.
$\{\mathrm{[Dy_{2}(L)_{2}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)]{\cdot}H_{2}O\}_{n}}$ (31, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}=2\mathbf{,}2$ -dimethylglutaric acid) is constructed by edge-sharing of dysprosium triangles to form extended dysprosium hydroxide ribbons.46 Compound 31 possesses two crystallographically independent DyIII ions with triangular dodecahedral geometries. A ribbon structure is formed from $\upmu_{3}{\bf-O H}^{-}$ -bridged ${\bf D y}_{3}$ triangles held together by edge-sharing. The ribbons are further connected by the ligands to construct a network. Ferromagnetic interactions are revealed by a dc susceptibility study while the magnetization dynamics are revealed by ac measurements. Compound 31 possesses thermally activated Orbach relaxation with an effective energy barrier of $78~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (112 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.42\times{{10}^{-8}}$ s in the high temperature region and direct and Raman processes at low temperatures. No hysteresis loop was found.
A ferromagnetically coupled dysprosium chain was assembled in the MOF $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{Dy}(\mathrm{TDA})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}$ (32, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{A}\ =$ thiophen-2,5- dicarboxylic acid).47 The eight-coordinate $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion exhibits a distorted bicapped trigonal prismatic geometry, completed by six carboxylate oxygen atoms and two water molecules. Three neighboring DyIII ions are bridged by carboxylate groups in a $\upmu_{1,3}$ -mode to generate a triangular $\{\mathrm{Dy}_{3}\}$ motif, which serves as a building unit to form a belt-like chain along the $b$ direction. The adjacent chains are further connected by $\mathrm{TDA}^{2-}$ ligands into a 3D framework. The increase in $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ in the low-temperature range indicates the presence of ferromagnetic coupling between $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions. Strong frequency dependence of the ac susceptibilities under zero dc field is observed, to give an effective energy barrier of $30.7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (44.2 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $2.4~\times~10^{-8}$ s. No hysteresis loop was reported.
## 3.2 Tuning the magnetization dynamics in MOFs
Framework structures can provide higher air/water/solvent and thermal stabilities than discrete molecules, based on which the local geometries of metal ions can be more conveniently perturbed via various physical and chemical methods. By taking advantage of structural diversity, molecules coordinating to the central metal ions can be introduced, released or exchanged, inducing structural perturbations of the MBBs. It has been proved that even noncoordinated solvents in the channels can influence the magnetization dynamics of the MOFs, in which SMMs serve as nodes.48 However, examples in this area are rather rare due to challenges associated with both isolation of the magnetically pure samples for magnetic measurements and complicated magneto-structural analysis.
3.2.1 Adjusting coordinated guests. The coordination environments of $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions in $[\mathrm{Dy}_{2}(\mathrm{FDA})_{3}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]_{n}$ (33, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{A}=$ furan-2,5-dicarboxylic acid) and $\mathrm{\small{[Dy_{2}(F D A)_{3}(D M F)_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]}_{{n}}}$ (34) can be fine-tuned by changing the solvents, resulting in enhanced energy barriers for magnetization reversal. $^{49}\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions in both 33 and 34 are eight-coordinate but exhibit slightly different coordination geometries. The coordination sphere of the DyIII ion in 33 is completed by seven carboxylate oxygen atoms and one DMF molecule, forming a $C_{2\mathbf{v}}$ coordination environment. The coordination polyhedra of the $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion in 34 can be described as $D_{4\mathrm{d}}$ symmetry. Dc susceptibility measurements of 33 and 34 under 1000 Oe suggest the presence of weak but non-negligible ferromagnetic interactions. Ac magnetic susceptibilities of 33 and 34 were measured at zero dc field. Both of these exhibit significant frequency dependence of the out-of-phase signals; however, no out-of-phase peaks are observed due to QTM. When a 2000 Oe dc field is applied, effective energy barriers of $29.1\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (41.8 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.08\times$ ${{10}^{-8}}$ s for 33 and $46.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (67.5 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $2.26\times10^{-11}$ s for 34 can be obtained. A further study of this system was performed on the $\mathbf{Y}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ diluted systems, $33(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{Y}$ and $\mathbf{34}\textcircled{\mathbf{2}}\mathbf{Y}$ . Dilution leads to a decrease in the external dc field needed to effectively suppress QTM. Under an optimized 300 Oe dc field, effective energy barriers of $25.7\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (37.0 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $8.24\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~s~}$ for $33(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{Y}$ and $32.0~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (46.0 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $1.46\times{{10}^{-7}}$ s for $\mathbf{34}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}}\mathbf{V}$ can be obtained. No hysteresis loops were observed for this system.
The coordination sphere of the $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion in $\{\mathrm{[Dy(BTC)(H_{2}O)]}\}$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{F}}_{\ensuremath{j}n}^{\phantom{}}$ (35, $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{TC}=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylic acid) is made up of six carboxylic oxygen atoms and one terminal water molecule, exhibiting a distorted pentagonal-bipyramidal geometry.50 Adjacent DyIII ions are connected by carboxylate groups to form a helical chain along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ direction. The chains are linked together through $\mathbf{BTC}^{3-}$ ligands to generate a 3D framework. Interestingly, the coordinated water and guest DMF can be removed by calcination of 35 under vacuum at $240^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to give another MOF, $[{\mathrm{Dy}}({\mathrm{BTC}})]_{n}$ (36). The $\mathrm{Dy}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion in 36 has unusually low six-coordination with a distorted trigonal prismatic geometry. Ac magnetic susceptibilities of 35 and 36 were measured at zero dc field. Compound 35 is paramagnetic but 36 exhibits frequency dependence of the ac signals. No peaks from out-of-phase signals are observed in 36 at zero dc field. Obvious maxima of the out-of-phase signals can be observed for 36 when an external dc field of 1000 Oe is applied, to give an effective energy barrier of $31.9\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (45.8 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $2.43\times$ ${{10}^{-6}}\ s$ . No hysteresis loop was reported for 36.
3.2.2 Influence of guest molecules on the magnetization dynamics of lanthanide SMM nodes in MOFs. $\{[\mathrm{Dy}_{2}(\mathrm{INO})_{4}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]$ 2solvent}n $\mathbf{\left(HINO=\right.}$ isonicotinic acid $N\cdot$ -oxide; solvent ${\bf\Lambda}={\bf D M F}$ for 37, MeCN for 38) containing $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{y}}_{2}$ binuclear SMMs as nodes shows a reversible single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformation between the two phases via guest solvent exchange, inducing a switch of the effective energy barrier for the two MOFs (Fig. 13).48 The porous framework features one crystallographically independent DyIII ion, which exhibits a monocapped square antiprism coordination sphere with approximately $C_{4\mathrm{v}}$ symmetry. The DyIII ions in 37 are surrounded by two oxygen atoms from a chelating ${\bf N O}_{3}^{-}$ and seven oxygen atoms from six different linkers. Two neighboring Dy centers are bridged to form a binuclear MBB. The MBBs are further linked by $\mathbf{INO}^{-}$ ligands to construct a 3D framework. The isomorphic phase, 38, can be obtained by soaking 37 in acetonitrile at room temperature. Dc magnetic susceptibility measurements of 37 and 38 indicate the presence of ferromagnetic interactions. Ac magnetic measurements at zero dc field were performed to give the effective energy barriers, with a negligible value for 37 and $76~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (110 K) with a $\tau_{0}$ of $3.24~\times~10^{-11}$ s for 38. Ab initio calculations imply that the different SMM behaviors arise not from a significant change in the intrinsic features of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{y}}_{2}$ nodes but rather from a slowing of the relaxation rate of incoherent quantum tunneling by two orders of magnitude due to the different dipole–dipole interactions, induced by the exchange of guest solvents. No clear hysteresis loop was observed at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ for 37 and 38.
## 4. Conclusions and outlook
The rapid development of MOF chemistry provides a powerful new platform for the study of molecular nanomagnets. We have detailed herein the advantages of MBBs in MOFs for magnetic studies, in which some of the MBBs cannot be achieved as discrete molecules but exhibit interesting magnetic properties. The almost infinite numbers of structural building blocks obtainable from a huge database of inorganic and organic species and their combination models, are the key point of this research area. The coordination geometries of the crystal fields (coordination symmetry and crystal-field strength) around the paramagnetic center play a key role in determining the magnetization dynamics. It has been demonstrated that perfect axial anisotropy, which can be achieved in particular crystal field symmetries, could largely improve the dynamic magnetic behavior of molecular lanthanide nanomagnets. Such axial conditions could be easily achieved in framework structures if the lanthanide ion could be made to lie in a prescribed position.
On the other hand, MBBs can be supported by framework structures to behave as different types of molecular nanomagnets, including SIMs, SMMs and SCMs with enhanced magnetic properties. Single metal ions or coordination clusters can serve as MBBs and can be arranged in a prescribed way within the framework structures. In other cases, they can be bridged into infinite chains behaving as SCMs. In such non-zero-dimensional arrays, not only the coordination geometries but also the magnetic couplings (exchange interactions and dipolar interactions) can be fine-tuned. In addition, the porous structures of MOFs can accommodate not only different guest molecules but also SMMs for enhanced SMM behavior.
Currently, the achievement of high energy barriers for magnetization reversal has been well demonstrated in several magnetically isolated lanthanide systems via a coordination symmetry approach; however, it is still rare to observe the hysteresis loops characteristic of magnets and, even when these are seen, this is at relatively low temperatures. Arranging the MBBs in ordered arrays has been shown to be advantageous in terms of the contributions of both the coordination geometry (to tune the energy levels) and magnetic interaction (to tune the relaxation rate) to the magnetization dynamics and the shape of hysteresis loops.
The combination of molecular nanomagnets and metal– organic frameworks presents great opportunities for the exploration of new molecular materials in both fields. Appropriate examples are still limited in this area and thus rational synthetic strategies are waiting to be developed. Based on the aforementioned results concerning constraining the coordination geometries of lanthanide ions and assembly of MBBs in MOFs, we propose two possible approaches that could be experimentally explored in the near future: (i) chemical modification of known SMMs to serve as ‘‘complex ligands’’ for the construction of magnetic MOFs; (ii) doping highly magnetic anisotropic metal ions into specific axial positions in diamagnetic frameworks and varying the doping concentration. These strategies will not only bring new samples to assist an in-depth understanding of the factors governing the magnetization dynamics of molecular nanomagnets but will also produce new research interest in MOF chemistry.
